-- A named site that leaves out an input with a TYPICALLY default takes the
-- default, unless another rule of the same name could also take what the site
-- writes. A rule could not take the site if a value the site writes does not
-- fit the type of that rule's input of that name; nothing else rules it out.
-- It need not be complete, and a name it lacks does not rule it out
-- (not-ok/tc/typically-overloaded-name.l4). When two rules could take the site
-- and neither is complete as written, no default is taken
-- (not-ok/tc/typically-overloaded-site.l4 and
-- not-ok/tc/typically-overloaded-fit.l4). When one of them is complete as
-- written, it is the one the site means, as it was before TYPICALLY.

-- 1. The types tell the rules apart. The site writes a NUMBER for `base`, which
-- the second `scaled` cannot take, so only the first can, and it takes its
-- default for `rate`.
GIVEN rate IS A NUMBER TYPICALLY 3
      base IS A NUMBER
GIVETH A NUMBER
scaled MEANS rate TIMES base

GIVEN base IS A STRING
      factor IS A NUMBER
GIVETH A NUMBER
scaled MEANS factor

#EVAL scaled WITH base IS 10                      -- 30: `rate` takes its default
#EVAL scaled WITH base IS 10, rate IS 2           -- 20: a value the call gives wins
#EVAL scaled WITH base IS "ten", factor IS 4      -- 4: the second `scaled`, every input written

-- 2. The same site inside a rule, where the value is a rule's own input and not
-- a literal.
GIVEN n IS A NUMBER
GIVETH A NUMBER
thrice MEANS scaled WITH base IS n

#EVAL thrice 10                                   -- 30

-- 3. A rule that is complete as written is the one the site means, so the other
-- rule's default is not taken. This answered 500 before TYPICALLY and still does.
GIVEN a IS A NUMBER
      b IS A NUMBER TYPICALLY 1
GIVETH A NUMBER
f MEANS a PLUS b

GIVEN a IS A NUMBER
GIVETH A NUMBER
f MEANS a TIMES 100

#EVAL f WITH a IS 5                               -- 500: the second `f`, as written
#EVAL f WITH a IS 5, b IS 2                       -- 7: only the first `f` has a `b`

-- 4. Three rules whose first inputs differ in type. All three have an input
-- named `x`, but only the first takes a NUMBER, so it is the only one the site
-- fits, and it is incomplete by one input that has a default.
GIVEN x IS A NUMBER
      y IS A NUMBER TYPICALLY 4
GIVETH A NUMBER
g MEANS x PLUS y

GIVEN x IS A STRING
      z IS A STRING
GIVETH A STRING
g MEANS z

GIVEN x IS A BOOLEAN
      w IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
g MEANS w

#EVAL g WITH x IS 3                               -- 7: `y` takes its default
#EVAL g WITH x IS "a", z IS "b"                   -- "b"
#EVAL g WITH x IS TRUE, w IS FALSE                -- FALSE
